description:
FILED s470bn (Opus, 2026-09-05), found building the `json-rt` macro row.

REPRODUCER (scratch/s470bn/jdbg.pl):
    use JSON::PP; my $j = JSON::PP->new->canonical;
    print $j->encode({ a => 1, b => "x" }), "\n";
    print $j->encode([1, 2.5, "s"]), "\n";
    print $j->encode({ v => [map { $_ + 1 } 1..3] }), "\n";
  perl 5.40.3:  {"a":1,"b":"x"} / [1,2.5,"s"] / {"v":[2,3,4]}
  PCL:          {"a":"1","b":"x"} / ["1","2.5","s"] / {"v":["2","3","4"]}

JSON::PP decides number-vs-string by asking whether the SV carries a numeric
flag (its `value_to_json` looks at the dualvar/POK-IOK state).  PCL's box does
not answer that question the way perl's SV does, so every number encodes
quoted.  A string that LOOKS numeric is correct on both sides ("x" stays "x"),
so this is the IV/PV distinction, not a coercion bug.

WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND JSON: any module that branches on "was this value ever a
number" sees the same answer.  `docs/ir-spec.md` Â§2.2's box model is where the
decision belongs; the `nv-ok` slot already exists and is what %p-storable-raw
consults for the dualvar split.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE BENCH: the `json-rt` macro row's document is ALL STRINGS
deliberately, so the two engines do the same work and print the same sum.  Fix
this and the row can carry numbers.
